# Line endings.
#
# Every text file is LF, in the index AND in the working tree, on every platform.
# `eol=lf` overrides core.autocrlf (Git for Windows ships it as `true` in the system
# gitconfig), so a Windows clone checks out LF too. That keeps the working tree in
# step with what AI tools, sed, python scripts and Linux containers write: a tool that
# rewrites a file whole can't silently flip its line endings, `git status` doesn't show
# phantom changes, and `git blame` history survives. Visual Studio, VS Code and Rider
# all edit LF files natively; .editorconfig tells them to create new files as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one exception: cmd.exe mis-parses a batch file that arrives LF-only.
*.bat          text eol=crlf
*.cmd          text eol=crlf

# Explicitly binary. Git's heuristic already spares most of these, but under a blanket
# `text=auto` an explicit marker is worth more than a heuristic.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.dll    binary
*.exe    binary
*.pfx    binary
*.snk    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
